'''''Tradescantia cerinthoides''''', commonly called '''flowering inch plant''', is a species of plant in the family [[Commelinaceae]] that is native to south east [[Brazil]] to north east [[Argentina]]. Described in 1843 by the German botanist, [[Carl Sigismund Kunth]]. The pink tipped white flowers and green purplish foliage are appealing features to gardeners. This plant is used in many parts of the world as an [[Ornamental plant|ornamental]], and can become an [[invasive species]].<ref name=plantnet>{{cite web|author = |title=Tradescantia cerinthoides|url=http://plantnet.rbgsyd.nsw.gov.au/cgi-bin/NSWfl.pl?page=nswfl&lvl=sp&name=Tradescantia~cerinthoides|work=PlantNet -New South Wales Flora Online|publisher=|access-date=June 21, 2020}}</ref><ref name=kew>{{cite web|author = |title=Tradescantia cerinthoides|url=https://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:173245-1|work=Kew Science, Plants of the World Online|publisher=|access-date=June 21, 2020}}</ref>

